[Music]  welcome to getting started with artifact  registry  today i'll show you how to deploy a  container stored in an  artifact registry repository to google  kubernetes engine  if you're new to artifact registry i  recommend you check out the last episode  of getting started  or the documentation before proceeding  with that in mind  let's walk through deploying to gke now  there's a good chance that if you're  watching this video  you already know about google kubernetes  engine but to get everyone up to speed  let's have a quick overview  gke is an enterprise grade platform for  containerized applications  including state full and state lists ai  and ml  linux and windows complex and simple web  apps  api and backend services you can use gka  to leverage  industry-first features like 4-way  auto-scaling and no stress management  you can also optimize gpu and tpu  provisioning  use integrated developer tools and get  multi-cluster support from sres  so why use artifact registry then with  gke  using artifact registry with gka gives  you the ability to manage  all of your artifacts in one place so  for example  you can build maven packages push them  to artifact registry  containerize them with cloud build and  jib and then deploy them  to gke all while using consistent im  across the build  process artifact management and gke  artifact registry also enables you to  run in fully regionalized stacks  so with artifact registry an australian  or south american customer can run gk  clusters  and manage container images in the same  region and pay  no egress costs while they pull images  pretty sweet huh so without further ado  let's deploy to gke first start with new  or existing code  here's a simple go hello world app that  i'm going to use in my container  you can also take a look at my docker  file here for kicks  so it's pretty much boilerplate see  below for a link to the documentation  where you can see  this file in detail i'm using cloud  build to build this project  but keep in mind that you don't have to  using cloud build allows you to  automatically push the image to artifact  registry  which i like because it streamlines the  deploy process  these next couple steps are for building  with cloud build  if you don't already know your cloud  project id you can get it  by running this command now create a  file called  cloudbuild.yaml here's the contents of  that file  but don't worry about rushing to pause  the video so you can copy this down  you can find this code in the  documentation which of course is linked  below  these variables repository and image are  used for the repository name  and the name of the image in the  repository so that they can be replaced  at build time  using custom cloud-build substitutions  this is very useful if you want to use  the same build config file to push  images to repositories  for different environments such as  testing staging and or production  for example this command would  substitute drain pipe for the repository  name  and my image for the image name  i run this command to submit my  container to build using cloud build  it is then stored using artifact  registry in the drainpipe repository  now that you have a project built and  ready to go you may need to configure  access to your container  check the docs to see the minimum  required gke versions to create clusters  that have default permissions to pull  containers from docker repositories in  the same project  if you are not using one of these gk  versions you will need to configure  access to pull containers using  kubernetes image full secrets  if you need to access containers from  repositories in another project then you  need to grant  read permission on that repository to  the identity being used to pull the  image  once that access is configured you can  run an artifact registry image on a gk  cluster  using this command where name is the  name you've given the object  location is the regional or  multi-regional location of your  repository  project id is of course your google  cloud project  id repository is the name of the  repository where the image is stored  image is the name of the image in the  repository and tag is the tag for the  image version that you want to pull  so for my drain pipe project i'd use  this command and now my image that's  stored in an artifact registry  repository  is deployed to gke want to see more on  deploying to google kubernetes engine  with artifact registry  check out the artifact registry quick  start for docker and the artifact  registry guide on integrating with  google kubernetes engine  do you have any questions about artifact  registry feel free to comment below  or tweet at me thanks for watching and  i'll see you  on the next getting started with  artifact registry

